# Python Development Standards

## Version Compatibility
All code in this project must be compatible with Python 3.12 or higher. Avoid using deprecated features or syntax that may not work in these versions.

## Project Structure
- Maintain a modular folder structure
- Group related functionality in appropriate packages
- Use clear, descriptive names for modules, classes, and functions
- Keep files focused on a single responsibility

## Code Quality
- Write descriptive docstrings for all modules, classes, and functions
- Include inline comments for complex logic
- Follow PEP 8 style guidelines
- Use type hints to improve code readability and assist with static type checking
- Keep functions small and focused on a single task

## Dependencies
- All dependencies must be listed in [requirements.txt](mdc:requirements.txt)
- Use specific version numbers to ensure reproducibility
- Regularly review and update dependencies
- Avoid unnecessary dependencies that could be replaced with standard library alternatives
- Consider security implications when adding new dependencies

## Documentation
- Keep [README.md](mdc:README.md) updated with:
  - Project overview and purpose
  - Installation instructions
  - Usage examples
  - Configuration details
  - Development setup
  - Contribution guidelines

## Development Workflow
- Use Git for version control
- Write comprehensive commit messages
- Create feature branches for new development
- Maintain a clean git history

## Testing
- Write unit tests for all functionality
- Aim for high test coverage
- Tests should be fast and independent

## Security
- Never commit secrets or credentials
- Use environment variables for configuration
- Follow security best practices for web APIs and data handling
